  {
   "id": "R7QR_dV_EQAJ",
   "from": "Sarah Weil",
   "author": "1c1a92f98ab395af",
   "date": "2021-02-11T11:08:46Z",
   "subject": "Re: fixnodes/fossil constraints in trait-dependent models",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div>For all those in the future who might be struggling with the same issue as me: I think I misunderstood how exactly nodes are constrained in the models. It turns out that you can&#39;t suggest probabilities for range occupancies in the past, but you can just select a single range. So instead of giving probabilities to $fixlikes as I had done in my first try (<span style=\"font-weight:400;font-style:normal\"><span><span>BioGeoBEARS_run_object$fixlikes &lt;- <span style=\"font-weight:400;font-style:normal\"><span><span>c(0, 0.47, 0, 0.53)) I just had to do: <span style=\"font-weight:400;font-style:normal\"><span><span>BioGeoBEARS_run_object$fixlikes &lt;- <span style=\"font-weight:400;font-style:normal\"><span><span>c(0, 0, 0, 1) and everything worked!</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div><span style=\"font-weight:400;font-style:normal\"><span><span><span style=\"font-weight:400;font-style:normal\"><span><span><span style=\"font-weight:400;font-style:normal\"><span><span><span style=\"font-weight:400;font-style:normal\"><span><span><br></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></div><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div dir=\"auto\" class=\"gmail_attr\">Sarah Weil schrieb am Montag, 8. Februar 2021 um 21:26:20 UTC+1:<br></div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><div>Just a little side note: I tried fixing the root node in a simple DEC model, and the resulting probabilities of range occupancies did not match 100% the probabilities I had given to begin with (with BioGeoBEARS_run_object$fixnode). The difference is not gigantic (0.51 becomes 0.46; and 0.48 becomes 0.51 for the major ranges) but just enough to change the principal ancestral range. Is this normal behaviour or have I missed something?</div><div><br></div><div>Any help is greatly appreciated.</div><div><br></div><div>Best,</div><div><br></div><div>Sarah<br></div><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div dir=\"auto\" class=\"gmail_attr\">Sarah Weil schrieb am Montag, 8. Februar 2021 um 14:47:59 UTC+1:<br></div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><br><div>Hi everyone,</div><div><br></div><div>I was just wondering if the possibility to constrain nodes to specific areas/trait states has been implemented in the trait-dependent version of BioGeoBEARS?</div><div><br></div><div>I&#39;ve been trying to use fixnode and fixlikes as described for the general BioGeoBEARS models, but it&#39;s not working for me.</div><div><br></div><div>A little description of what I&#39;ve done:<br></div><div>- I&#39;ve tried a very simple case with a 1area geography and nullrange included. First, I ran the model with all parameters fixed except t12 and t21 (trait transition rates). This resulted in a relprobs matrix (<span style=\"font-weight:400;font-style:normal\"><span><span>res$ML_marginal_prob_each_state_at_branch_top_AT_node) with 4 columns. (I&#39;m assuming here the following: column 1 = species present in null range + trait state 1; column 2: species present in region1 + trait state 1; column 3: species present in nullrange + trait state 2; column 4: species present in region 1 + trait state 2).</span></span></span></div><div><span style=\"font-weight:400;font-style:normal\"><span><span>This resulted in the following probabilities for the root node:</span></span></span></div><div><span style=\"font-weight:400;font-style:normal\"><span><span>c(0, 0.05, 0, 0.95).</span></span></span></div><div><span style=\"font-weight:400;font-style:normal\"><span><span><br></span></span></span></div><div><span style=\"font-weight:400;font-style:normal\"><span><span>- Then I tried constraining the root node as described for normal models, so I did</span></span></span></div><div><span style=\"font-weight:400;font-style:normal\"><span><span>BioGeoBEARS_run_object$fixnode &lt;- root_node</span></span></span></div><div><span style=\"font-weight:400;font-style:normal\"><span><span>BioGeoBEARS_run_object$fixlikes &lt;- <span style=\"font-weight:400;font-style:normal\"><span><span>c(0, 0.47, 0, 0.53)</span></span></span></span></span></span></div><div><span style=\"font-weight:400;font-style:normal\"><span><span><span style=\"font-weight:400;font-style:normal\"><span><span>I ran the model (no error messages), but the root node in the results had the following probabilities: <br></span></span></span></span></span></span></div><div><span style=\"font-weight:400;font-style:normal\"><span><span><span style=\"font-weight:400;font-style:normal\"><span><span><span style=\"font-weight:400;font-style:normal\"><span><span><span style=\"font-weight:400;font-style:normal\"><span><span>c(0, 0.04, 0, 0.